{"qid":"Q1039","names":["São Tomé and Príncipe"],"title":"São Tomé and Príncipe","endonym":{"native":"República Democrática de São Tomé e Príncipe","lang":"pt"},"capsule":"The Democratic Republic of São Tomé and Príncipe (República Democrática de São Tomé e Príncipe) is a small island nation in the Gulf of Guinea, comprising two main volcanic islands off the equatorial coast of Central Africa, uninhabited before Portuguese colonizers arrived around 1470. Portugal transferred sovereignty on 12 July 1975 following negotiations after the Carnation Revolution; the Movement for the Liberation of São Tomé and Príncipe (MLSTP) under Manuel Pinto da Costa governed initially as a one-party Marxist state. In 1990 São Tomé became one of the first African states to adopt multiparty democracy, holding free elections in 1991, and has since maintained one of Africa's most consistent electoral records. The country's economy depends primarily on cocoa exports and, increasingly, tourism and anticipated offshore oil revenues.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. As a contemporary sovereign state, São Tomé and Príncipe's territory and political status are precisely defined by international law; the attestation window 1976–2025 reflects its post-independence existence with full confidence.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: São Tomé and Príncipe","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Sao-Tome-and-Principe"},{"label":"Wikipedia: São Tomé and Príncipe","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%A3o_Tom%C3%A9_and_Pr%C3%ADncipe"}],"britannica":"place/Sao-Tome-and-Principe","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"São Tomé and Príncipe","rulers":[{"qid":"Q504522","name":"Miguel Trovoada","start":1991,"end":1995,"image":"Miguel Trovoada in 1995.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Miguel%20Trovoada%20in%201995.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"CC BY 4.0","artist":"European Commission, European Communities","attributionRequired":true}},{"qid":"Q1287504","name":"Leonel Mário d'Alva","start":1991,"end":1991,"image":"Leonel Mário d'Alva 1977.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Leonel%20M%C3%A1rio%20d'Alva%201977.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"CC BY 4.0","artist":"European Commission, European Communities","attributionRequired":true}},{"qid":"Q1161572","name":"Daniel Daio","start":1991,"end":1992,"image":null},{"qid":"Q1563807","name":"Norberto Costa Alegre","start":1992,"end":1994,"image":null},{"qid":"Q289154","name":"Manuel Quintas de Almeida","start":1995,"end":1995,"image":null},{"qid":"Q688513","name":"Armindo Vaz d'Almeida","start":1995,"end":1996,"image":null},{"qid":"Q709756","name":"Raul Bragança Neto","start":1996,"end":1999,"image":null},{"qid":"Q956967","name":"Guilherme Posser da Costa","start":1999,"end":2001,"image":"Guilherme Posser da Costa 2018 (cropped).jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Guilherme%20Posser%20da%20Costa%202018%20(cropped).jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"Public domain","artist":"Óscar Medeiros (VOA)","attributionRequired":false}},{"qid":"Q867590","name":"Gabriel Costa","start":2002,"end":2002,"image":"Gabriel Arcanjo Ferreira da Costa 2014.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gabriel%20Arcanjo%20Ferreira%20da%20Costa%202014.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"Public domain","artist":"Office of the White House (Amanda Lucidon)","attributionRequired":false},"nativeName":"Gabriel Arcanjo Ferreira da Costa","nativeLang":"pt"},{"qid":"Q454046","name":"Maria das Neves","start":2002,"end":2004,"image":"Maria